APPR-2660	EXECUTION	dfab8a7c-cdb4-47f2-9210-82cc3d7c7bd9	Incident	High CPU on a Kubernetes control plane node typically originates from kube-apiserver overload (excessive LIST/WATCH requests), runaway kube-controller-manager reconciliation loops, etcd compaction storms, or kube-scheduler thrashing. This SOP provides a systematic diagnostic workflow to identify the offending component via process-level metrics, service logs, Kubernetes API metrics, and etcd health checks, followed by targeted service restarts or etcd compaction if safe.	Execute SSH Remediation	85	REJECTED	2026-08-04 19:13:58.346	{"model": "nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b", "agentId": "agent-unix-resolver-01", "kbTitle": "SOP: High CPU Utilization on Kubernetes Control Plane Node", "targetCi": "control plane (192.168.100.101)", "agentName": "🤖 Unix Auto-Resolver Agent", "riskLevel": "HIGH", "department": "DevOps Team", "aiReasoning": "Control plane CPU spikes are usually caused by a single component misbehaving. The diagnostic steps isolate the process (ps/top), correlate with service logs (journalctl), and expose Kubernetes internal metrics (apiserver /metrics) to distinguish between client-driven load (high request rates/latency) and internal loops (controller-manager, scheduler). Etcd endpoint status reveals compaction backlog or disk latency. Remediation is component-specific: restarting apiserver clears in-memory caches and drops heavy watches; restarting controller-manager breaks reconciliation storms; etcd compaction reclaims space and reduces CPU spent on MVCC. Safety checks ensure cluster quorum and API availability post-action.", "safetyChecks": [{"check": "Verify all control plane systemd units show 'active (running)' via: systemctl is-active kubelet kube-apiserver kube-controller-manager kube-scheduler etcd", "passed": true}, {"check": "Confirm etcd cluster health: ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki